{"type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"template_override": 1, "type": "audio", "id": 1, "title": "", "contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"audiosrc": "engenn8le17ob04re2aud01", "coverimg": "engenn8le17ob04re2img05.jpg", "images": [{"image": "engenn8le17ob04re2img05.jpg", "text": "", "time": "0", "id": 0}, {"image": "engenn8le17ob04re2img05.jpg", "text": "Our reader mailbag: Linda from Seattle writes, \"Don't you think that most superstitions are just common sense? I mean, not walking under a ladder seems pretty practical to me.\"<br/><br/>Some make the mistake that superstitions are just common sense.\nNot walking under a ladder seems more logical than other superstitions like: don't let a black cat cross your path, saying \u201cbless you\u201d when someone sneezes or that bad luck comes in threes. But some make the mistake. However, there is some back story to this superstition.", "time": "100", "id": 1}, {"image": "engenn8le17ob04re2img02.jpg", "text": "As with many superstitions, the origins of the ladder come from religious belief. The belief is that God is made up of three parts, the Holy Father, the Holy Son, and the Holy Ghost. This is the Trinity. Christians believe that the number three is somewhat sacred and that the triangle formed by a ladder that leans against a building is symbolic of the Trinity. Walking through this triangle was seen as breaking the Trinity and a sin.", "time": "34000", "id": 2}, {"image": "engenn8le17ob04re2img02.jpg", "text": "Thus, many Christians must take this into consideration if they choose to walk under a ladder. So you see, this superstition is not as trivial as it may seem. Although, walking under a ladder while someone is working on it may not be the best time to break the Trinity anyways, as a falling object could be the start of a bad day for you. So both things seem correct.\n<br/><br/>So the next time you are running your triathlon or riding your tricycle watch out for the Holy Trinity and workers overhead.", "time": "63000", "id": 3}]}}], "_data": []}], "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"template_override": 1, "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": " I can talk about manners, customs and superstitions.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can say and use words like triangle.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can pronounce words with the same consonant sound.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " What do you think common sense means?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": " Do you think superstitions are just common sense? Why or why not?", "type": "Free Header Item"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct", "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}]}, "type": "feedback"}, {"content": " Ask your partner:", "type": "free_header_text"}, {"content": "Pronunciation Spot:  Common sense vs. Superstition", "type": "free_type_text_area"}], "img_path": "", "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "id": 0, "stimuli_ids": [0]}, {"template_override": 1, "type": "STI", "questions": [{"responses": [{"correct": 0, "_data": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}], "selected": 0, "id": 0}, {"correct": 1, "_data": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}], "selected": 0, "id": 1}], "_data": [{"type": "question", "contents": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}]}], "id": 0}], "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": " \nSally discusses the walking under a ladder superstition and how it relates to the Holy <span class=\"tool-tip\" id=\"tip_1_0\">Trinity</span>.\n", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Content", "type": "group_name"